#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Weekly estate report, written on lexie, interpreted by Claude, mailed to the owner.

Covers the previous Monday-to-Monday week in REPORT_TZ, for the whole estate:
  - incidents (Grafana's alert history) and automated actions (the action log);
  - backups: sally, the off-site copy, both restore tests;
  - the lab and ruby and jennie, from Prometheus: availability, reboots, disk,
    probes, certificates, patching, failed units, pools, WAN, alerting;
  - mail and web on ruby, from its logs in Loki.

The figures are computed here, and every check that finds something wrong is an
issue. Claude then reads a summary of them, never addresses, client IPs or
message details, and writes the interpretation at the top. It has no tools. If
it cannot be reached the report still goes, with the issues and a note.

"Needs attention" is followed up before the mail goes (follow_up): an issue that
matches a runbook rule goes to the runbook engine, and up to INVESTIGATE_MAX go
to the responder, which investigates read-only and reports as it does for
alerts. The mail gives each issue its status and does not wait for the results.

  estate-weekly                 mail the report for the week that just ended
  estate-weekly --stdout        print it instead, and follow nothing up
  estate-weekly --no-claude     skip the interpretation
  estate-weekly --week-ending 2026-11-16
"""
import hashlib
import argparse
import collections
import datetime as dt
import json
import os
import re
import smtplib
import socket
import ssl
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import tomllib
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from email.message import EmailMessage
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

